    <statement><p>Compute the length of the curve <m>{\bf r\it(t)} = \left\lt  -2 t,4 t+ 1,-3 t  - 5\right&gt;</m> over the interval <m>0 \le t \le 5</m></p>
<p><m>L=</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>26.9258</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>x(t)=-2 t,y(t)=4 t + 1, z(t)= -3 t  - 5</m> hence
<me>x'(t) = -2,\qquad y'(t)=4,\qquad z'(t)=-3</me>
We use the Arc Length Formula to obtain:



<me>L=\int_0^{5}\|(\bf r\it'(t)\|\,dt=
   \int_0^{5}\sqrt{x'(t)^2+y'(t)^2+z'(t)^2}\,dt</me>



<me>=\int_0^{5}\sqrt{(-2)^2+4^2+(-3)^2}\,dt=
   5 \sqrt{29}</me>



</solution>
